(* ============================================================================
   PART 11: CANONICAL SOURCE WRITER (NORMATIVE)
   ============================================================================

   The canonical writer (`carve fmt`, the `carve` render target) serializes a
   document back to Carve source.

   1. THE INVARIANTS. For any document x:

        parse(fmt(x)) == parse(x)        -- meaning is preserved
        fmt(fmt(x))   == fmt(x)          -- the writer is idempotent

      The first is about the AST, not the bytes: `fmt` MAY normalize the
      author's spelling (indentation, marker alignment, escape form) but MUST
      NOT change what the document says. The second forbids growth -- a value
      that gains an escape per pass violates it, which is how over-escaping is
      usually first noticed.

      EQUALITY IS MODULO ESCAPING -- NORMATIVE. `escaped_text` and `text`
      compare EQUAL, and an adjacent run of them compares as the single text
      node holding the same characters in the same order. `a\-b` and `a-b`
      are the same document for the purpose of this invariant, and differ only
      in what the author wrote to get there.

      Without that clause the first invariant would be unattainable by
      construction. §5 requires the writer to escape `"` and `'`
      UNCONDITIONALLY: a bare quote reaching the writer as text would otherwise
      re-derive as smart punctuation (PART 9 §8) and change the document, so a
      text node holding a quote MUST come back carrying an escape, which parses
      to `escaped_text`. It is also the comparison §4 already performs
      internally -- the two renders it weighs differ precisely in their
      escaping, so telling them apart BY the escaping would escalate every
      document.

      What escaping still MUST preserve is the rendered result: the escape is
      authoring syntax, so `to_html(fmt(x)) == to_html(x)` admits no such
      latitude, and neither does idempotence.

   1a. THE INVARIANT OUTRANKS THE PER-CONSTRUCT RULES -- NORMATIVE. §1 states
      what the writer must ACHIEVE; §2 through §11 state how it SPELLS each
      construct. Where spelling a construct by its own rule would emit a
      document that does not re-parse to the tree it was written from, §1 wins
      and the per-construct rule yields. A writer that deviates for that reason
      is CONFORMANT: its output must not be corrected back to the literal
      reading of the rule it departed from, and the deviation is not a defect
      to be filed against it.

      THE TEST IS ON THE EMITTED BYTES, not on the source and not on the
      construct in isolation. Whether an emitted run opens what it appears to
      open is a property of the WHOLE output document: an opener's meaning
      depends on what precedes it, an opener that needs a closer is not one
      without it, and the same three characters at byte 0 open something they
      do not open on line 12. So what an implementation owes is to read its own
      OUTPUT with its own parser. Pattern-matching the SOURCE cannot answer the
      question -- the source is the document that already round-trips, and the
      bytes at risk are the ones the writer just produced.

      THE INVARIANT IS §1's, WHICH IS THE PARSE FORM. `parse(fmt(x)) ==
      parse(x)` is the requirement. `to_html(fmt(x)) == to_html(x)` is a
      consequence of it and is strictly weaker -- §2a already says that holding
      is necessary, not sufficient -- so a writer satisfying only the HTML form
      still fails this section.

      IT IS NOT A LICENSE TO RESPELL. §2a and §6 are unchanged. What this
      section permits is the SMALLEST departure that restores the invariant,
      and another spelling of the SAME construct is preferred over a different
      construct that happens to render alike. Dropping content, re-wrapping
      prose or swapping a construct for a synonym are not available here: each
      of them changes the document, which is the thing §1 forbids.

   1b. A FLATTEN PRESERVES THE BOUNDARY IT DISSOLVES -- NORMATIVE. §1a settles
      what a producer owes where §1's invariant CAN hold. This settles what it
      owes where the invariant cannot hold at all.

      A slot that takes INLINE content only -- a caption line, a fence title, a
      table cell, an image's alternative text, a definition term -- cannot
      carry blocks. A producer handed block content for such a slot therefore
      FLATTENS it, and the flatten is lossy by construction: the emitted
      document holds fewer blocks than the tree it was written from, so
      `parse(fmt(x)) == parse(x)` is unattainable here and §1 is not the rule
      that applies. What the producer still owes is the BOUNDARY.

      THE RULE. Where two former sibling blocks each contribute at least one
      TOKEN to the slot, the producer MUST emit a separator between them, and a
      separator is sufficient IF AND ONLY IF re-reading the emitted slot draws
      no token from both sides of the join: no word, no delimiter run, no
      construct delimiter. Stated as a property and not as a list of the pairs
      that collide, because the pairs are not enumerable -- every inline
      construct Carve has, and every one it gains, can end one block and begin
      the next.

      A BLOCK THAT CONTRIBUTES NOTHING IS NOT A SIDE, which is why the rule is
      written over contributed tokens rather than over sibling positions.

        <figcaption><p>a</p><p></p><p>b</p></figcaption>

      holds three blocks and one join: the empty one puts no token in the slot,
      so there is no boundary to protect beside it and a separator there would
      be whitespace no author wrote. The emitted caption is `a b` and not
      `a  b`. This is not the conditioning refused below -- it asks whether a
      side exists at all, not what characters happen to sit on one.

      THE FAILURES ARE ONE FAILURE, and only some of them have a visible face.
      A `<figcaption>` holding two paragraphs flattens with the boundary lost:

        <p>one</p><p>two</p>                    ->  onetwo
        <p><strong>a</strong></p>
        <p><strong>b</strong></p>               ->  *a**b*
        <p><code>a</code></p>
        <p><code>b</code></p>                   ->  `a``b`

      THE UNIT IS THE TOKEN, NOT THE NODE, and that difference is the whole
      rule. A node test passes `onetwo` and `one two` alike -- both are a
      single `text` node -- while the boundary a reader recovers from one and
      cannot recover from the other is a token boundary. So the test is stated
      where the loss is.

      IT IS §23's TEST READ BACKWARDS. PART 9 §23 states the forward half for a
      LINE boundary: ONE boundary produces ONE node, "however the boundary is
      spelled". A flatten is that situation arriving from the other side. The
      block boundary leaves no node either, because the slot has nowhere to put
      one, so it survives only in the BYTES -- and the bytes are read by a
      tokenizer.

      THE CANONICAL SEPARATOR IS ONE SPACE (U+0020), emitted at every such
      boundary and conditioned on nothing further. A space ends a word and ends
      a delimiter run, and it is neither punctuation nor alphanumeric, so it
      cannot combine with the characters on either side of it into a construct
      neither block wrote. The emitted forms of the three documents above are
      `one two`, then `*a* *b*`, then the two code spans separated the same
      way. Conditioning it on the neighbouring CHARACTERS -- emitting it only
      where a collision looks likely -- is the source-pattern test §1a already
      refuses: the question is about the bytes the producer is about to emit,
      and a producer able to answer it is equally able to just emit the space.

      A TEXT character that becomes a live delimiter after the join is instead
      an ESCAPING question governed by §2 through §1a. This clause supplies the
      space and nothing else.

      The producer MUST flatten rather than omit the content. It MUST NOT write
      an escape such as `*a*\*b*`: the defect is per-boundary, not
      per-character, and §2 permits no invented character.

      WHAT IS NOT REQUIRED. The flatten need not be REVERSIBLE. Two paragraphs
      written into a caption come back as one paragraph and no spelling of the
      slot restores them, because the slot forbids the structure. A producer
      exposing conversion diagnostics SHOULD report that structural loss, as
      PART 12 §14 requires for a dropped short caption. The requirement here is
      bounded at the boundary: the words and the constructs on either side of
      it survive as themselves.

   1c. A WRAPPER ITS OWN CONTENT SPELLS AWAY IS A DECLARED CEILING --
      NORMATIVE (carve#1658). §1b settles what a producer owes where an
      inline-only slot forces a flatten. This settles the other place §1's
      invariant cannot hold, and it is the mirror image: not a slot that
      cannot HOLD what it was handed, but a wrapper whose own content, written
      out, IS the thing the block layer reads instead of it.

      THE RULE. Where a block's whole content is a single node, and that node's
      own spelling AT THE BLOCK'S OWN COLUMN is read back as a block opener of
      that node's kind, the writer emits that spelling and the wrapper is LOST.
      `parse(fmt(x)) == parse(x)` is unattainable for such a document, so §1 is
      not the rule that applies and §1a's smallest-departure licence has
      nothing smaller to reach for -- every spelling of the content is the
      content's own. What the producer owes instead is that the loss is
      DECLARED and not silent:

        - A PRODUCER WITH A DIAGNOSTIC CHANNEL REPORTS IT. The code is
          `structure-unspellable`, which `docs/html-import.md` already defines
          for exactly this and already makes the single carve-out to that
          page's own `parse(htmlToCarve(h)) == htmlToAst(h)`. The AST-returning
          exit loses nothing and reports nothing; the source-writing exit
          reports the row.
        - A PRODUCER WITH NO CHANNEL STATES THE CEILING IN ITS CONTRACT.
          `carve fmt` and the `carve` render target have no diagnostic list and
          can only throw, so what they owe is that their stated contract NAMES
          this shape as one it does not cover. A contract that promises the
          invariant without qualification, while carrying an exception nothing
          declares, is worse than a narrower contract that is true as written:
          every reader of the first one is entitled to rely on it.

      STATED OVER WHAT THE SHAPE SPELLS, never over the node type, the same way
      §10j is. Two shapes have the property today, and they share no node type,
      no vocabulary list and no reason beyond the spelling: a `paragraph` whose
      whole content is one `image`, which writes `![a](u)` and reads back as
      the standalone image; and a `paragraph` whose whole content is one
      `comment`, which writes `%% c` and reads back as the block comment.
      `image` is in the INLINE vocabulary and `comment` is in the BLOCK one
      (docs/profiles.md), so a rule written over the types would have had to
      name them one at a time. A block whose content spells anything ELSE -- a
      second node beside it, a text run, a NO-BREAK SPACE (§7) -- keeps its
      wrapper and no ceiling is reached.

      DECIDED ON THE SPELLING, NEVER ON RESOLUTION -- and PART 9R R7 does not
      change that. R7's promotion phase reads the RESOLVED tree; this clause
      reads what a shape SPELLS, and the two are deliberately independent. A
      writer that consulted resolution would write the same paragraph
      differently depending on whether a definition appears somewhere else in
      the document -- the reason `paragraph.blockImage` (PART 12 §23) is a
      field the writer does not read. So this clause's ceiling is reached by
      `![a](u)` and by `![a][r]` alike, defined or not.

      THE CEILING IS UNIFORM AND NOT POSITIONAL, which is the half that decides
      the shape rather than merely describing it. At top level the image
      paragraph does have a spelling -- one leading space, which the block
      layer reads as a paragraph and not as a standalone image -- and the
      writer still does not use it. Inside a list item or a definition
      description there is no such spelling at any width: the marker's own
      content column absorbs the padding, so `- ![a](u)` and `-       ![a](u)`
      are the same tree, and `: ` behaves the same way. A writer preserving the
      shape at one nesting level and losing it at the next, with nothing
      declaring the difference, is not a preservation rule; and the indented
      form emits meaning-bearing leading whitespace, which editors and
      pipelines strip. So the wrapper is lost EVERYWHERE.

      This exit REPORTS the wrapper loss rather than refusing the tree, as
      `docs/html-import.md` requires, preserving an editor round trip through
      trees the renderer accepts.

      THE WRAPPER IS STILL LOST, and this clause does not pretend otherwise. It
      bounds the loss TO the wrapper -- the content, its attributes and its
      neighbours all survive as themselves -- and it puts the loss on the
      record, which is the whole of what §1 can be held to here.

      A CHECKER FOR §1 NARROWS TO THIS BOUND AND NO WIDER -- NORMATIVE
      (carve#1679). The paragraph above says what this clause forgives. This
      says what it does NOT, because a checker asserting `parse(fmt(x)) ==
      parse(x)` has to encode both halves, and stated only as a permission the
      carve-out acquired one mechanism per engine that reached it -- three
      engines, three exemptions, none of them written where the next reader
      would look.

      THE RULE. The two trees MUST agree once every wrapper this clause names
      is dissolved into its content, on BOTH sides. Nothing else is forgiven: a
      dropped node, a reordering, a changed attribute and a changed node type
      all still fail, and so does the dissolution of a wrapper that carries
      anything of its own. A wrapper carrying an attribute block is not BARE --
      losing it would lose the attributes this clause says survive -- and
      neither is a node that owns a label, a destination, a level or a kind.

      A RESOLUTION RESULT ABOUT THE WRAPPER IS NOT CONTENT OF IT -- NORMATIVE
      (carve#1823). `paragraph.blockImage` (PART 12 §23) is the ONE field a
      wrapper may carry and still be BARE, and it is admitted BY NAME. §23
      bounds it to `true` and PART 9R R7's promotion phase is its only
      producer, so what it records is that this paragraph IS the block image --
      which is what the dissolved spelling states outright. The two spellings
      §23 blesses for that one fact, an `image` in block position and a
      `paragraph` carrying the field around one `image`, differ in no
      information, so dissolving either into the other takes nothing with it.
      This bounds the CHECKER's comparison and not the writer: `fmt` does not
      read the field, per DECIDED ON THE SPELLING above.

      IT IS NOT A LICENSE TO IGNORE UNKNOWN KEYS. A key no clause has ruled on
      is content, and a count of keys cannot tell the two apart. A wrapper is
      bare only where the field's KEY, its VALUE and the CHILD it describes all
      hold: `blockImage: false`, the field beside an attribute block, the field
      around a `comment`, and any other field are each NOT bare, and a writer
      that lost one of them still fails.

      WHAT DOES NOT CONFORM is a carve-out keyed on the difference EXISTING.
      "The two trees came back different, and a fixture records the second one,
      so accept it" forgives a dropped node exactly as readily as a lost
      wrapper, and the fixture is then evidence of nothing. The clause is the
      definition; a fixture is at most an instance of it.

      WHERE THE SET IS SOURCED FROM IS FREE. One engine states a predicate over
      what the shape spells and is bounded by construction, another derives the
      set from the corpus documents whose pinned canonical form re-parses
      differently and applies the bound explicitly. Both conform. The bound is
      the rule; the sourcing is not.

      A CHECKER THAT WIDENS THE BOUND CANNOT DETECT ITS OWN WIDENING, so the
      width is pinned separately or it is not pinned at all. The dissolution is
      applied to BOTH trees, so widening it can only ever HIDE a difference and
      never create one, and a corpus sweep stays green under any widening
      whatsoever.

   2. THE ESCAPING RULE -- NORMATIVE. A character is escaped IF AND ONLY IF
      omitting the escape would change the re-parsed AST.

      Escaping MORE than that is a defect, not a safe default. It is how a
      formatter turns `50% faster: yes (ok)` into `50\% faster\: yes \(ok\)`,
      which re-parses identically -- so nothing there needed escaping.

      Escaping LESS is a correctness bug: the document changes meaning.

      THE UNIT IS THE OPENER, NOT THE CHARACTER. Where a construct opens on a
      RUN of characters, the whole run is escaped. `--` opens an en dash
      (PART 9 §8), and `\--` suppresses it just as `\-\-` does; the escaped
      form of a suppressed opener is the whole opener escaped. A half-escaped
      run breaks as soon as the surrounding text shifts what the run abuts, and
      `\.\.\.` versus `\...` is the same question with the same answer.

      So the escape decision is taken per OPENER OCCURRENCE: would omitting the
      escapes on this occurrence let the construct form? Yes -> escape the run.
      No -> emit it bare.

      AND THAT HOLDS INSIDE A UNIT THAT ESCALATED -- NORMATIVE (carve#1533).
      One occurrence needing the conservative form does not put the OTHER
      occurrences in the unit with it. §2b bounds how far the fallback reaches;
      the reach is not the only thing that was too wide. An indented paragraph
      whose text reads `{.note}` is written

        \{.note}
        This paragraph.

      and not `\{\.note\}`. The `{` is load bearing: bare, at column zero,
      the first line opens a block attribute the document does not have. The `.`
      and the `}` beside it open nothing at those positions, and neither does
      the `.` ending the second line, so by the test above they are emitted
      BARE -- in the same unit, in the same line, beside the escape that stayed.

      A WRITER MAY STILL BE UNABLE TO DECIDE AN OCCURRENCE, and §2b's fallback
      is what it falls back to -- per occurrence now rather than per unit. A
      fallback that spends more escapes than this test asks for is wider than
      the minimum and never narrower, which is the only direction §1 tolerates.

   2a. THE WRITER DOES NOT SUBSTITUTE ONE CONSTRUCT FOR ANOTHER -- NORMATIVE.
      §2 governs ESCAPING. It does not license rewriting a construct into a
      different one that happens to render the same, and neither does §1's
      invariant: `to_html(fmt(x)) == to_html(x)` holding is necessary, not
      sufficient. Two spellings that render alike are still two spellings, and
      removing that is what a CANONICAL writer is for.

      These counterexamples render identically but re-parse to different ASTs:

        source     written as    what changed
        `* %%`     `* +`         a line comment became the CONTINUATION MARKER
                                 (§17 L3), a different construct with a body
        `| %%%`    `| %% %`      a comment-block fence (§28) split into a line
                                 comment plus text

      The second is also what §2's own THE UNIT IS THE OPENER paragraph
      forbids one layer down: `%%%` is an opener run, and half of it plus a
      stray character is exactly the "shape that happens to work rather than
      one that says what it means".

      The escaping half remains §2's test: omit an escape unless doing so
      changes the re-parsed AST.

   2b. THE SCOPE OF AN ESCALATION IS THE SMALLEST UNIT THAT FAILS --
      NORMATIVE. A writer that cannot decide an occurrence exactly may fall
      back to the conservative form (§4). That fallback reaches the SMALLEST
      UNIT whose minimal form fails to re-parse -- the INLINE RUN, or the
      BLOCK containing it -- and NOT the whole document. Outside that unit,
      every candidate character is emitted by §2's own test, which for a
      character nothing needs means BARE.

      A paragraph the author indented so that its text reads `## H`, followed
      by a paragraph reading `plain (b) text`, is written as

        \#\# H

        plain (b) text

      The first paragraph's minimal form opens a heading the document does not
      have, so that block escalates. The second paragraph's minimal form comes
      back as itself, so it does not, and its parentheses stay bare. Writing
      `plain \(b\) text` there is what §2 already calls a defect rather than a
      safe default -- the escape changes nothing, and it is in the document
      only because a DIFFERENT block needed one.

      THE COMPARISON STAYS DOCUMENT-SCOPED, WHICH IS THE OTHER QUESTION. §4
      rules out re-parsing a LINE on its own, because a line has lost the
      document's link-reference and footnote definitions and reports a
      difference escaping never caused. That argument is about what the writer
      PARSES in order to decide; this section is about which BYTES the decision
      then selects.

      THE UNIT NESTS, AND IT IS TRIED SMALLEST FIRST. The inline run carrying
      the occurrence is tried before the block that holds it. A failure that
      is not confined to the run widens to the block, which is the usual case
      for a block opener at column 0: what such a run opens is a property of
      the LINE it begins, not of the run. Widening then re-runs §1a's test on
      the emitted bytes, because escalating a unit has changed them, and it
      terminates -- a document in which every block escalates is the
      conservative form, arrived at because every block needed it rather than
      because one did.

      IT DOES NOT LOOSEN §2 ANYWHERE. A unit that fails is escaped in full, by
      §2's THE UNIT IS THE OPENER: `\#\# H` and not `\## H`. What narrows is
      the reach of the fallback, not the strength of the escape it applies.

   3. WHY A STATIC CHARACTER TABLE CANNOT IMPLEMENT §2. Whether a character
      is significant depends on the line, not on the character:

        `[`  literal alone, an opener in `[a](b)`
        `(`  literal in `[a] (b)`, an opener in `[a](b)`
        `^`  literal at column 0 before a space, an opener in `^[note]`
        `-`  literal mid-word, a list marker at column 0, an en dash doubled
        `_`  literal alone, an opener only when a closer follows on the line

      A writer that decides per character with no context must therefore
      over-escape to stay correct, which is the defect §2 forbids.

   4. A PERMITTED STRATEGY -- NOT THE PINNED OUTPUT. The output is pinned by
      §2 and by §2 alone: a character is escaped if and only if omitting the
      escape would change the re-parsed AST. This section describes ONE way to
      compute that, which an implementation MAY use and is NOT required to.
      Where the two differ, §2 wins.

      §2 is the rule worth keeping, because it is the one an author can see the
      difference in, and because §3's argument does not lead here: it shows a
      static character TABLE cannot implement §2, not that per-character
      decisions are impossible.

      The procedure, then, as a strategy:

      W1 Render the document twice: the MINIMAL form, escaping only the
         UNCONDITIONAL set (§5), and the CONSERVATIVE form, additionally
         escaping every CANDIDATE-set character (§5).
      W2 If the two are byte-identical, emit either.
      W3 Otherwise parse BOTH and compare the resulting documents, ignoring
         source positions and key order.
      W4 Equal -> emit the minimal form. Different -> emit the conservative
         form FOR THE SMALLEST UNIT THAT FAILS (§2b), and the minimal form
         everywhere else, re-running W3 on the result.
      W5 Inside a unit that stayed conservative, offer each OPENER OCCURRENCE
         its bare form and keep the bare form wherever W3 still holds (§2).
         A unit is not one knob: the occurrences that survive escaped are the
         ones that failed, not the ones that shared a block with a failure.

      A WRITER MAY STOP THE SEARCH SHORT, and §2b's fallback is what it returns
      to when it does: the occurrences it did not get to stay escaped. That is
      WIDER than §2's minimum and never narrower, so a spent budget costs
      idle escapes and never correctness. It is also why the state a bounded
      search returns is still checked by W3 against the conservative form's own
      re-parse rather than assumed.

      Two forms and one comparison, with the choice made by the parser rather
      than by a table -- so the writer cannot drift from the grammar as the
      grammar grows.

      THE COMPARISON IS DOCUMENT-SCOPED. A smaller scope loses reference and
      footnote definitions and can report differences escaping never caused,
      so W1 and W3 render and parse whole documents.

      THAT IS NOT A REASON TO ESCALATE THE WHOLE DOCUMENT, and reading it as
      one is what §2b now forbids. Where the two parses differ, they differ
      SOMEWHERE, and W4 takes the conservative spelling only for the smallest
      unit that fails.

      THE TWO RENDERS ARE COMPARED WITH EACH OTHER, not with the source tree.
      Some permitted writer forms re-parse to a different tree while rendering
      identical HTML; comparing with the source would conflate those differences
      with escaping.

   5. THE TWO SETS.

      UNCONDITIONAL -- always escaped in a text node, never re-derivable:
        the backslash itself; the backtick (opens a code span in any
        position); and `"` / `'`. A BARE quote re-derives as smart
        punctuation (PART 9 §8), so a quote that reached the writer as TEXT
        is one the author escaped, and it stays escaped. A quote carried by a
        `smart_punctuation` node is NOT text: the writer emits that node's
        source run BARE, which is what makes `fmt` reproduce the author's
        spelling.

        The CARET is also unconditional, for a different reason: it opens
        nothing on its own, but its escape carries information the AST records
        separately. A text node whose LEADING caret came from an escape is
        marked as such, so an image followed by a caret line stays a paragraph
        instead of being promoted to a figure. Comparing that mark in W3 would
        escalate every document whose text begins with a caret; ignoring it
        would silently turn the image case into a figure. Escaping the caret in
        BOTH forms keeps the two renders identical on that point, at the cost
        of one escape on a character that is rare in prose.

        THAT COST IS THE STRATEGY'S, NOT THE OUTPUT'S -- NORMATIVE, and the
        difference is §4's own. This section names the sets W1 renders
        against, so a caret named here is escaped in BOTH forms and W3 never
        weighs the mark; it is not a license to EMIT an escape §2 does not
        ask for. §4 pins the output on §2 and yields to it wherever the two
        differ, and §10g already restates the reach in those terms -- "§5's
        unconditional set escapes a LEADING caret in TEXT". So "always escaped
        in a text node" is the set's membership rule, and §2 is the test the
        emitted byte answers to.

        THE MARK IS THE PROMOTION, NOT A FIELD, which is why the LEADING case
        needs no exception to §2. `resources/ast-schema.json` declares no
        field for it: what the escape changes there is whether the block comes
        back a PARAGRAPH or a FIGURE, and that is a difference §2 already sees.

        SO THE LEADING CASE IS UNMOVED. An image over `\^ not a caption` keeps
        the backslash; bare, the same two lines are a `figure` with a
        `figcaption`. That is §2 requiring the escape, not §5 excusing it.

      CANDIDATE -- escaped only when W4 selects the conservative form:
        `* _ ~ / = ,`        emphasis and the braced pair delimiters
        `[ ] ( ) { } <`      links, images, spans, attributes, autolinks
        `@ #`                mentions and tags (§7 boundary rules); `#` also
                             opens a heading at column 0
        `> + - . ) : | % !`  block openers at column 0, plus the inline forms
                             `%%`, `::` and the symbol shortcode `:name:` (the
                             inline-footnote `^[` is covered by the
                             unconditional caret above)
        `$`                  math

      A character in neither set is never escaped.

      The set must cover EVERY construct opener, or W4 has nothing to fall
      back to and the document is unrecoverable: `\@user` would emit as
      `@user`, W3 would correctly see a `mention` where the source had text,
      and W4 could not fix it. When a new opener is added to the grammar, its
      character belongs here.

   ============================================================================ *)
